See Home Depot.com slash price match for details. It's no surprise that we homo sapiens tend to have a human-centric view of intelligence. When we think of smart animals, we generally think of primates or other animals close to us on the evolutionary tree. But octopuses shake up this view of the world. They haven't shared a common ancestor with humans in at least 600 million years. But somehow, these invertebrates evolved remarkable problem-solving abilities, curiosity, and an intelligence all their own. So what do we know about how octopuses think and how they experience the world? How and why did they evolve to be so curious and smart? How are their brain structured?
详情请参见 Home Depot.com/pricematch。毫无疑问，我们智人对智能的看法往往是以人类为中心的。当我们想到聪明的动物时，通常会想到灵长类动物或其他在进化树上与我们相近的动物。但章鱼打破了这种世界观。它们与人类至少有 6.000 万年没有共同祖先了。但不知何故，这些无脊椎动物进化出了非凡的解决问题能力、好奇心以及独一无二的智力。那么，关于章鱼是如何思考以及它们是如何体验世界的，我们知道些什么呢？它们是如何以及为什么进化得如此好奇和聪明的？它们的脑结构是怎样的？

3
00:01:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
And what can we learn about thinking and intelligence in general from studying how octopuses behave? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. My guest today is Dr. Jennifer Mather, a professor of psychology at the University of Lethbridge in Alberta, Canada, and one of the world's leading experts on octopus behavior. Dr. Mather has spent decades studying cephalopods, marine animals that include octopuses, cuddlfish, and squids. Her fieldwork has taken her around the world to places including Bermuda, Hawaii, Bon Air, and the Pacific Island, Moria. She's authored dozens of academic papers and is co-author of the book Octopus, The Ocean's Intelligent Invertebrate. She was also the scientific advisor to the Netflix film My Octopus Teacher, which won the 2021 Academy Award for Best Documentary.
从研究章鱼的行为中，我们能对普遍的思考和智能学到什么？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》（Speaking of Psychology），这是美国心理学协会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。我今天的嘉宾是詹妮弗·马瑟（Jennifer Mather）博士，她是加拿大阿尔伯塔省莱斯布里奇大学的心理学教授，也是全球顶尖的章鱼行为专家之一。马瑟博士花了几十年时间研究头足类动物，这类海洋动物包括章鱼、墨鱼和鱿鱼。她的实地考察足迹遍布全球，包括百慕大、夏威夷、博内尔岛以及太平洋岛屿莫雷阿岛。她撰写了数十篇学术论文，并合著了《章鱼：海洋智能无脊椎动物》（Octopus, The Ocean's Intelligent Invertebrate）一书。她还是 Netflix 电影《我的章鱼老师》（My Octopus Teacher）的科学顾问，该片荣获 2021 年奥斯卡最佳纪录片奖。

4
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:43.000
And if you haven't watched it, you really should. Dr. Mather, thank you for joining me today. It's good to be here. And I mentioned in the introduction that octopuses are very far removed from us on the evolutionary tree. so far removed that some people compare meeting an octopus to meeting an alien life form. What do you think of that analogy? Oh, I'm not really happy about that. Let's say that life on this planet is very, very diverse.
如果你还没看过，真的应该去看一看。马瑟博士，非常感谢你今天能来。很高兴来到这里。我在引言中提到，章鱼在进化树上离我们非常遥远。远到有人将与章鱼相遇比作与外星生命相遇。你对这个类比有什么看法？哦，我其实不太喜欢这种说法。应该说，这个星球上的生命是非常非常多样化的。

5
00:02:43.000 --> 00:03:20.000
So octopuses are completely not like us. On the other hand, honeybees are completely not like us, and they're not like octopuses either. So octopuses give us a glimpse into diversity. Why do you believe that octopus has evolved to be so intelligent? What were the evolutionary pressures at work? Probably the evolutionary pressures were the competition of the bony fishes. Because the bony fishes evolved about the time that the chileoids cephalopods evolved. And bony fishes really now dominate the ocean.
所以章鱼和我们完全不像。另一方面，蜜蜂和我们完全不像，它们和章鱼也不一样。因此，章鱼让我们得以一窥多样性。你认为章鱼为什么会进化得如此聪明？当时起作用的进化压力是什么？进化压力可能来自硬骨鱼的竞争。因为硬骨鱼进化的时候，刚好也是蛸亚纲头足类动物进化的时候。现在硬骨鱼确实主宰了海洋。

6
00:03:20.000 --> 00:04:03.000
They're fast, they're smart, they're maneuverable. And to keep up with them, the cephalopods had to, evolve, if not necessarily to be fast, maybe to be smart. Do you have any salient examples of behavior that show how smart or curious octopuses are? I mean, what can they do that might surprise people? But one of the things that everybody quotes is what they call the coconut carrying octopuses in Australia. And a group of octopuses dug out, separated coconut halves from the muddy sand. and wandered off across in the open area, carrying them underneath them.
它们速度快、聪明、灵活。为了跟上它们的步伐，头足类动物不得不进化，即使不一定非要变得速度快，也许也得变得聪明。你有什么突出的行为例子可以说明章鱼有多聪明或多好奇吗？我的意思是，它们能做哪些可能会让人们大吃一惊的事情呢？但每个人都会引用的事情之一，就是他们在澳大利亚所说的“搬运椰子的章鱼”。一群章鱼从泥沙中挖出、分理出对半开的椰子壳。然后把它们顶在身底下，一路蹒跚着穿过开阔区域。

7
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:52.000
And after a while, when they got to the point that they wanted to stop and rest, they dropped the two halves, separate them, neatly line them up, crawl inside, pull it over between them, I guess you'd call it, and find themselves a perfect shelter. That's really interesting because that's something that has been called mental time travel, which is thinking about how the world is going to be in the future and making plans for what to do about it. That's pretty amazing, and that reminds me of a scene in my octopus teacher where the octopus that was basically the star of the show created a kind of armor using old shells and seafloor detritus, and it appeared to be very deliberate behavior to protect itself from a predator shark.
过了一会儿，当它们走到想停下来休息的地方时，它们放下两半椰子，把它们分开，整齐地排成一行，然后爬进去，拉过椰子盖在自己身上——我想你可以这么叫它——为自己找到了一个完美的庇护所。这真的很有趣，因为这被称为“心理时间旅行”，也就是思考未来世界会是什么样，并为之做出计划。这太神奇了，这让我想起了《我的章鱼老师》中的一个场景：作为该片绝对主角的那只章鱼利用旧贝壳和海底碎屑制作了一种盔甲，这似乎是一种为了保护自己免受捕食性鲨鱼侵害的蓄意行为。

8
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:36.000
We often hear that there are very few animals that use tools like we humans do, but does this behavior and the coconut behavior indicate that octopuses are also using tools? Oh, absolutely. And one of the ways in which it's really clear is that they will hide in some kind of shelter. And of course, the shelters have different sizes of entrances as well as different spaces behind. And way back when I was studying octopuses in Bermuda, I was trying to figure out. how you could characterize their shelters. And one thing I noticed is the bigger the entrance, the more likely the octopus was to bring rocks and shells and pile them up in front of it to make the entrance smaller.
我们经常听到，很少有动物像我们人类那样使用工具，但这这种行为以及搬运椰子的行为是否表明章鱼也在使用工具呢？哦，绝对是的。其中一个很明显的证明方式就是，它们会躲进某种庇护所里。当然，庇护所的入口大小以及后面的空间也各不相同。很久以前，我在百慕大研究章鱼时，我曾试图弄清楚你该如何描述它们的庇护所。我注意到的一件事是，入口越大，章鱼就越有可能把石头和贝壳搬来堆在入口前面，好把入口变小。

9
00:05:36.000 --> 00:06:11.000
And there's tool use. But actually, my favorite tool use of theirs is they have a flexible siphon or funnel, which is really exhaloned as it would be if we blew our water. and they use this water jet for a whole bunch of things. They use it to clean out holes. They use it to repel nuisances, not really big predators, but nuisances, which includes, of course, my roping hand when I'm feeling around their home and trying to figure out what they've had for lunch yesterday.
这就是工具的使用。但事实上，我最喜欢它们使用工具的方式是，它们有一个灵活的虹吸管或漏斗，这其实就像我们喷水时排出的水流一样。它们用这个水流喷射来做很多事情。它们用它来清理洞穴，用它来驱赶讨厌的家伙，不是那种真正的捕食大敌，而是讨厌的东西，当然这其中也包括当我把手伸进它们的家、试图摸索它们昨天午餐吃了什么时，我那只被它们当成绳索抓的手。

10
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:54.000
So one of my favorite anecdotes, I really have to tell you this one, they have the giant Pacific octopus pre-will all the time at the Seattle Aquarium. And these animals are active at night, and they prefer that it's dark at night. And there was one situation where they had the night light on over top of the octopus tag. And the octopus simply reached up with its siphon, jetted a jet of water at the light, and shorted it out. What the heck? You have to wonder how it figured out that it. could do that. My guess is that they would have a general habit of jetting water nuisances
所以，我最喜欢的轶事之一——我真的必须告诉你这个——西雅图水族馆一直养着巨型太平洋章鱼。这些动物在夜间活动，而且它们更喜欢夜里黑漆漆的。有一次，章鱼展池上方开着一盏夜灯。这只章鱼竟然直接用它的虹吸管对准灯喷射了一股水柱，把灯给弄短路了。搞什么鬼？你不得不惊叹它是怎么想出自己能做到这一点的。我猜它们本来就有一种习惯，会朝讨厌的东西喷水。

11
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:32.000
like pesky researchers and scavenging fish. And there are reports of octopuses in labs that have done things like squirted at researchers that maybe they didn't like for some reason. I mean, it was always the same person, right? When there's one octopus was doing that. Let me ask you, are we anthropomorphizing them or do they have distinct personalities? Well, there's two questions there. The first question is, can they tell individual humans apart? And with my colleague Roland Anderson at the Seattle Aquarium, we did conduct tests to see whether they could recognize individual humans.
比如讨厌的研究人员和食腐鱼类。据实验室里的章鱼报告，它们做过一些事情，比如向它们可能出于某种原因不喜欢的研究人员喷水。我的意思是，总是同一个人，对吧？当时有一只章鱼在这么做。请问，我们是在拟人化它们，还是它们拥有鲜明的个性？嗯，这里有两个问题。第一个问题是，它们能分辨出不同的人类吗？通过与我在西雅图水族馆的同事罗兰·安德森（Roland Anderson）合作，我们进行了测试，看看它们是否能识别不同的人类。

12
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:53.000
And the answer was, yes, they can. And by the way, the two subjects, one of whom, of course, was Ron, dressed in the same Seattle Aquarium T-shirt. So that clue wasn't available to them. And we didn't actually figure out what clues were available, although there were different shapes and sizes. Still, that's pretty good. They can tell us apart.
答案是，是的，它们能。顺便说一句，两个测试对象（其中一个当然是罗恩）穿的是同一件西雅图水族馆 T 恤。所以这个线索对它们来说是没有用的。而且我们实际上并没有弄清楚它们利用了哪些线索，尽管形状和大小有所不同。尽管如此，这已经相当厉害了。它们能分清我们。

13
00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:22.000
Now, you've written that octopuses are tremendous explorers and that they really have an insatiable desire for moving around. I mean, they just don't stay in one place. Can you talk about that? What does that look like in the wilder, even in an aquarium? What happens to an octopus that is an explorer and is confined? Well, I'm really jealous, actually, of the octopus arms. because they've got eight of them, and they can explore separately with every one of them.
现在，你曾写过章鱼是极好的探险者，它们确实有着永无止境的到处移动的渴望。我的意思是，它们就是不会呆在一个地方。你能谈谈这个吗？在野外甚至在水族馆里，这看起来是什么样的？一只生性爱探险却被关起来的章鱼会发生什么？嗯，其实我真的很嫉妒章鱼的触手。因为它们有八条触手，每一条都可以独立探险。

14
00:08:22.000 --> 00:09:03.000
So actually, when they're hunting, they're not actually hunting by fission. They go to likely places, and then they stick their arms and crevices and snake them along cracks and explore the algae by sort of fueling up the stems and down the leaves. And so all the time, they're picking up information through their suckers. By the way, each sucker has hundreds of chemical and tactile. and each arm has hundreds of suckers. So they've got tons and tons of information. Speaking of information and how octopuses gather it, I mean, they're clearly using their arms for a lot of this work, but they do have brains. In fact, I understand they have more than one brain.
所以实际上，当它们捕猎时，它们并不是通过分裂来捕猎的。它们会去那些可能藏有猎物的地方，然后将触手伸进石缝中，顺着裂缝蜿蜒前行，通过在茎部上下搜寻来探索藻类。就这样，它们一直在通过吸盘收集信息。顺便说一下，每个吸盘都有数百个化学和触觉感受器。每条触手都有数百个吸盘。所以它们拥有海量的信息。说到信息以及章鱼是如何收集信息的，我的意思是，它们显然用触手完成了大部分工作，但它们确实有大脑。事实上，据我所知，它们不止一个大脑。

15
00:09:03.000 --> 00:10:03.000
I mean, how is the octopus brain organized? No, no, it's a popular fallacy. They have one central brain. But what this comes from is the fact that only two-fifths of the nerves of the octopus are in the brain. Three-fifths are out there in the arms, and each arm, in fact, has a chain of ganglia down its dorsal surface. Each one of these ganglia is actually centered over one of the suckers. And though there's a sucker ganglion, this chain of ganglia is really, really responsible for making sure the suckers do what they're supposed to do and feel and smell what they're supposed to do, okay? But they're not a brain. One thing to call them is a plexus, and a plexus is a series of same units, which does have some organization, but it doesn't have a
我的意思是，章鱼的大脑是如何组织的？不，不，这是一个普遍的误解。它们只有一个中央大脑。但这种说法源于这样一个事实：章鱼只有五分之二的神经在大脑中。五分之三在触手上，事实上，每条触手的背侧表面都有一串神经节。这些神经节中的每一个实际上都位于一个吸盘的正上方。虽然有一个吸盘神经节，但这串神经节确实非常负责任地确保吸盘做出它们应该做出的反应，并感受和嗅出它们应该感知的东西，好吗？但它们不是大脑。称呼它们的一种方式是“丛”（plexus），而“丛”是一系列相同的单元，它确实具有某种组织结构，但它没有一个。

16
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:37.000
central controller. Another way to look at it is what's going on, and the arms is subroutines. So the octopus brain doesn't tell the arms what to do in detail. Somebody described it as the octopus brain tells the ganglia what to tell the arms to do. There's an evolutionary reason for this, and I have to back up a little bit. Octopuses have no bones. And when you think about it, if you had no bones, how could you possibly move whole things, move parts of you around?
中央控制器。看待这个问题的另一种方式是，在触手中发生的事情就像是子程序。所以章鱼的大脑并不会向触手详细下达具体怎么做的指令。有人将其形容为：章鱼的大脑告诉神经节该命令触手做什么。这背后有一个进化的原因，我得稍微倒回来说一下。章鱼没有骨头。仔细想想，如果你没有骨头，你怎么可能移动整个身体，或者移动身体的各个部分呢？

17
00:10:37.000 --> 00:11:12.000
And the answer to that is specifically in the arms that the octopus stiffens some of the muscles to act as temporary skeletons. And then the other muscles articulate against those temporary skeletons. It's really way more flexibility than we have. But if you think about it, you couldn't possibly program all that in the brain. So what the brain does is it sends out general signals to the ganglia and the ganglia go, oh, okay, I'm supposed to do this.
这个问题的答案具体就在于章鱼的触手上，它们收紧一些肌肉来充当临时的骨骼。然后其他肌肉以这些临时骨骼为支点进行运动。这确实比我们拥有大得多的灵活性。但仔细想想，你绝不可能在脑子里把所有这些都编程好。所以大脑所做的就是向神经节发出一般性信号，而神经节就会想：“哦，好的，我该这么做。”

18
00:11:12.000 --> 00:12:04.000
So it's true that the arms have a lot of autonomy. There are octopuses, long-armed octopuses, that actually have autonomy that they can cast off an arm if they're threatened. And one of these cast-off arms will actually crawl down your hand, and the suckers will make contact with whatever is underneath them. There's a rumor. I've never seen this, but anyway, first of all, I should say that if an item out there in the environment is not terribly interesting and small, the octopus may reach out an arm, hold onto it with the suckers at the tip, and passes sucker by sucker by sucker by sucker up to the mouth to decide whether it's useful in hand or good to taste.
所以，触手确实具有很大的自主性。有些章鱼，比如长腕章鱼，在受到威胁时甚至真的可以自主舍弃一条触手。其中一条被舍弃的触手实际上会沿着你的手爬行，吸盘会与它底下的任何东西接触。有个传闻。我从来没见过这个，不过无论如何，首先我应该说，如果环境中的某个东西既不是非常有趣又很小，章鱼可能会伸出触手，用尖端的吸盘抓住它，然后一个接一个、一个接一个地把这个东西往上递到嘴边，以决定它在手里是否有用，或者尝起来味道是否好。

19
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:52.000
and I've done an ethogram, which is a description of all the behaviors of the octopuses. And in this particular case, I called this action conveyor belt. But the rumor is, and I haven't seen this, but the rumor is that an arm that has been cast off from the body can pick up a sucker and pass it from sucker to sucker to sucker towards the month that isn't there anymore. So that's a lot of organization, but it's not a brain because it's not a decision maker. And for me, subroutines is the best way to think about it. This isn't exactly a psychological question, but since you know so much about cephalopods, and I don't, how do octopuses camouflage themselves so well?
我曾制作过一份行为谱（ethogram），也就是对章鱼所有行为的描述。在这个特定的例子中，我把这个动作叫做“传送带”。但传闻是——我还没亲眼见过，但传闻说——从身体上脱落下来的一条触手可以捡起一个吸盘，然后一个接一个、一个接一个地把东西朝着那个已经不存在的嘴巴方向传递。所以这里面有大量的组织结构，但它不是大脑，因为它不是决策者。对我来说，“子程序”是思考这个问题的最佳方式。这不完全是一个心理学问题，但既然你对头足类动物了解这么多，而我并不了解，章鱼是怎么把自己伪装得这么好的呢？

20
00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:23.000
My understanding is that they have very limited color vision. So what does it work when they do this? Well, it's terribly ironic. They do not have color vision, and yet they can use color and camouflage. And exactly how they do it, we do not know. It's one of the big paradoxes of octopus behavior. But they have fabulous camouflage. And the reason they have such good camouflage is they have many organs in the skin, chromatophores.
据我所知，它们的色觉非常有限。那么它们在做这件事时，原理是什么呢？嗯，这极其具有讽刺意味。它们没有色觉，然而它们却能利用颜色进行伪装。它们究竟是如何做到这一点的，我们并不知道。这是章鱼行为中最大的悖论之一。但它们的伪装堪称一绝。它们之所以能伪装得这么好，是因为它们的皮肤里有许多器官——色素细胞（chromatophores）。

21
00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:55.000
Each of those chromatophores is an elastic sack containing pigments that are either yellow or red or brown black. And each of those elastic sacks has muscles pulling it out. So when the muscles relax, you can't see anything. You can see the reflective surfaces below. When they pull it out, suddenly you see the color. And the reason they're so good at this is, given that it's muscles, they could be very specific.
